            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "I built qtox for windows successfully, using the qtox windows-builder docker image, inside a linux VM.\n\nThere are actually very few steps involved, and it's also reasonably quick (about 10-20 minutes start to finish on my system). This requires about 8G of disk space, so ensure you have at least 10G free before starting.\n\nKeep in mind that this uses pre-build binaries of the dependencies (Qt, ffmpeg, etc), but it does build qtox.exe itself from source, and the resulting installer does include everything which is required to run and use it.\n\nI used Alpine linux as host (booted the tiny 65M live image `alpine-virt-3.22.0-x86_64.iso` in virtual box, without any SSD/HDD mounted, with 24G ram assigned - but it uses less than that. requires some initial setup like network/DHCP etc, which is out of scope for this guide).\n\nHowever, any system where docker works should work with the same instructions (except the alpine-specific docker install instructions), and probably even on windows too, if one has docker installed and using some POSIX shell (msys2, cygwin, busybox-w32, etc).\n\nThe resulting binaries seem to work fine for me on windows 10.\n\nThe qtox source dir remains on the host (the alpine linux VM in my case, if it persists), and qtox source files can be edited/updated on this host before building again, for instance.\n\nHere's my procedure. Feel free to suggest improvements, etc (e.g. an incremental build command which may complete quicker if it was already built before), but keep in mind that we want to keep it simple, and then people can modify it to their needs on their own.\n\n```sh\n# qtox-for-windows cross-build on Alpine linux.\n# should also work anywhere else with docker.\n\n# Alpine-specific: setup docker, if not installed (as root):\napk add docker\nservice docker start\n# rc-update add docker default  # optional: auto-start on boot\n\n# --- general instructions start here ---\n\n# create a host dir ~/qtox - to be mounted as $HOME inside the docker image\nmkdir ~/qtox\n\n# clone qtox and ci-tools in this dir (relatively small). these remain at the\n# host FS outside the docker image, can be edited manually at the host, etc:\ncd qtox\ngit clone https://github.com/TokTok/qTox.git\ngit clone https://github.com/TokTok/ci-tools.git\ncd ..\n\n# create the build-dir inside the qTox source dir. The build-script seems\n# to require that, but any name would work. we're using \"win64build\":\nmkdir -p ~/qtox/qTox/win64build\n\n# get windows builder docker image (downloads 1.5G + extract -> + 6G on disk)\n# the image includes pre-built mingw toolchain and qtox deps (toxcore, Qt, ...)\ndocker pull ghcr.io/toktok/qtox:windows-builder.x86_64\n\n# enter shell inside the image (user: root, HOME: /qtox, -v... mounts the host's dir as $HOME):\ndocker run -it -v ~/qtox:/qtox ghcr.io/toktok/qtox:windows-builder.x86_64 bash -l\n\n# inside the docker image shell, cd to the win64build dir at the host\ncd qTox/win64build\n\n# and build it. this takes few minutes, and adds ~ 2G at the win64build dir\nPROJECT_NAME=qtox /qtox/ci-tools/platform/windows/cross-compile/build.sh --src-dir /qtox/qTox --arch x86_64 --build-type Release\n\n# exit the docker image shell, back to the host\nexit\n\n# observe the resulting binaries at the host:\ncd ~/qtox/qTox/win64build\nls -l `find . -name \\*.exe | grep q`\n\n# output on 2025-08-07:\n#\n# -rwxr-xr-x    1 root     root      14239501 Aug  7 16:10 ./_build-win64/qtox.exe\n# -rwxr-xr-x    1 root     root      11914255 Aug  7 16:11 ./install-prefix/qtox.exe\n# -rwxr-xr-x    1 root     root      11914255 Aug  7 16:11 ./package-prefix/qtox/qtox.exe\n# -rw-r--r--    1 root     root      21242403 Aug  7 16:11 ./package-prefix/setup-qtox.exe\n#\n# The 1st is unstripped.\n# the 2nd and 3rd are stripped and identical.\n# the 4th is the setup binary, which can be extracted using 7z, and contains\n# in bin/ the stripped qtox.exe and all the required DLLs (Qt, ffmpeg, etc).\n```",
            "created_at": "2025-08-07T17:41:58Z",
